ich  roughly equates to around about a 60 to  70 increase in in in eu imports of lng  uh i think that's highly optimistic uh  given given current export capacities  and also uh eu import capacities of lng  um i think if they could reduce  dependence by maybe a third that would  be very good i think two-thirds is is  probably pushing it too far really and  is this i mean this is all about  sanctioning russia it's it's it's a  financial penalty for russia it's it's  aimed at causing economic harm to russia  ultimately though is is russia still  going to be able to sell its oil and gas  will there be buyers out there will it  be able to sell still sell at market  price is it really going to hurt russia  well i think undoubtedly in the long run  i think you know if russia loses access  to western markets it's lost access to  the us um if it also loses access to  europe as europe seems to be implying uh  you know that will will have um impact  on on russian uh on demand for russian  oil which should uh reduce the price  that they can pay i mean they're going  to be able to sell to to other countries  uh china uh in the asian countries and  so on um but but that sort of reduced  number of buyers will necessarily i  think uh reduce the the amount of price  they can pay i think that's true also  gas um as europe seeks to wean itself  off euro of russian gas um  russia is in the process of transiting  to china uh building new pipeline  infrastructure to to supply uh gas from  from the yamaha field down into down  into china but that's a massive  infrastructure effort that will take  some time to come online uh and also  china will be in quite a strong  bargaining position of course um you  know with russia not having many other  customers china will will be able to to  leverage that position to negotiate  themselves a bit of deal so  yeah in some i think it rushes the price  that they can  achieve for their energy exports will  will undoubtedly go down  toby it's been great 