 has been deferred by two  months so that does provide a little bit  of  leeway for the next few weeks but  overall sri lanka faces a very  challenging challenging situation on its  external uh external debt and its  foreign exchange reserves and the  government says that it can manage this  this situation on its own without  resorting to help from the imf is that a  wise strategy do you think so i would  think for sri lanka the most prudent  option now is to enter into negotiations  with with its creditors uh with a view  to restructuring its external debt to  restructure that uh you know in a  preemptive manner uh and such a  restructuring program would ideally be  underpinned and supported by an imf  agreement uh an imf agreement in that  situation would be able to unlock uh  some options of bridge bridge financing  that will help sri lanka to build up  reserves in the interim whilst it goes  through a debt restructuring process now  even if somehow sri lanka is able to  meet its maturing debt liabilities by  scraping together some swaps or  government transactions it's still doing  so at the expense of critical uh imports  for the economy imposed such as full  cooking gas and a number of other uh  imported commodities  as a result of which citizens are facing  a degree of hardship so really the best  balance from sri lanka's economic  perspective in the in the long term  would be to preemptively restructure its  external debt liabilities this an issue  that we've discussed many many times on  counting the cost how much of this  current crisis is due to the fact that  sri lanka is so indebted to china  so i would say that um  the current situation is not entirely  because of sri lanka's liabilities to  china so if you look at the upcoming  maturities over the next few years  typically around 20  of the maturing liabilities are  liabilities that are owed to uh or to  china uh the bulk of the  the bulk of liabilities actually arise  out of sri lanka's  borrowings from commercial markets or  from financial markets particularly  international sovereign bonds syndicated  loans and so on so whilst there is a  component of chinese  debt and chinese liabilities in this  in this equation i would not say that it  is the predominant cause of the  challenges sri lanka is facing right now  all right what so what is the cause we  talked a few moments ago about the now  scrapped policy to switch to 100 organic  farming  that  affected  exports and food security we talked  about the tea industry the biggest  export earner suffering is is there one  single cause or is just sri lanka in the  wrong place at the wrong time  economically at the moment so the the  the scrapping of um of chemical  fertilizer replacing with organic  fertilizer  that  we don't have evidence uh as yet to uh  to be able to attribute it to say the  decline in uh in t exports in in january  um survey data conducted by vedic  research does suggest that farmers do  expect significant uh decline in yield  so there would be uh some impact from uh  from that uh uh policy as well but sri  lanka's real problem is the fact that it  lost access to global credit markets  around early 2020 just before the the  the pandemic uh the pandemic hit by  losing access to global credit markets  it's been not been able to raise uh uh  uh capital to be able to refinance its  maturing debt so it has had to pay to  pay back debt using its reserves and  today reserves have as a result dwindled  down to the levels that we are at and  that's really the the crux of the  problem that sri lanka faces in foreign  exchange with sri lanka's tourism  industry taking such a big hit first of  all from the pandemic and now due to the  war in ukraine is there anything that  the government can do to to increase its  its  its foreign currency reserves you know  to earn  currency that it desperately needs  yeah so certainly the the point that  ukraine does have an impact on on sri  lanka's tourism which was just  recovering in the last few months um  around 25 percent of um  arrivals in the last few months have  been from uh either ukraine or russia uh  so the the the conflict that certainly  does have an impact on uh on the overall  balance of payments as does of course  the knock-on impacts that we're seeing  in global commodity prices particularly  uh particularly oil gas uh wheat and a  number of other uh important food  products as well because sri lanka is  the net importer so certainly the  current uh geopolitical challenges do  not work in sri lanka's favor in terms  of its uh in terms of its current  account that said the government has  taken some important positive steps in  in recent days it floated the rupee the  central bank floated the sri lankan  rupee a couple of days ago  and that certainly is a step in the  right direction towards encouraging more  inflows through formal uh banking  channels that can help the buildup of  reserve so that's certainly a positive  the central bank also recently uh raised  policy interest rates by 100 basis  points um at last week's monterey board  meeting again a positive in terms of  demand management and uh towards  building up reserves so there are steps  in the right direction there's still a  long way to go in terms of further  fiscal tightening further further moves  on the monetary side as well if that is  underpinned by an imf program and debt  restructuring i would think that is  really the way forward for sri lanka to  really sustainably build up its reserves  and to be able to both meet its external  debt repayments and provide the imports  that are required to sustain the economy  darshal it's been really good to talk to  you on county the cost many thanks  indeed for being with us  thank you very much  and that's our show for this week don't  forget you can get in touch with us if  you want to comment on anything that  you've seen you can tweet me i'm at a  finnegan on twitter please use the  hashtag ajctc when you do or you can  drop us a line counting the cost at al  jazeera.net is our email address there  is as always plenty more for you online  at aljazeera.com  ctc that takes you straight to our page  and there you'll find individual reports  links even entire episodes for you to  catch up on  but that is it for this edition of  counting the cost i'm adrian finnegan  from the whole team here in doha thanks  for being with us the news on al jazeera  is next

